  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM EDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nSynoptic scale troughing from the Hudson Bay area southward through \nthe eastern CONUS, along with upper ridging from the Four Corners \nregion through the northern Rockies, will continue through Friday. A \npattern change to a warmer and drier regime begins over the weekend, \nbut before that happens we'll have some scattered storms to deal \nwith this afternoon. More on that below. By Saturday morning the \nupper trough begins to pull away to the northeast and the western \nridge starts its eastward trek towards the southern Appalachian \nregion. The ridge should be centered over Tennessee by Monday \nafternoon, where it will remain through the end of the period, and \nlead to increasingly warm conditions across the NWS Morristown \nforecast area.\n\nGoing into a bit more detail, this afternoon we still have cyclonic \nflow aloft, with a shortwave slated to move through this afternoon \nand into the evening hours. This will push a weak front our \ndirection, which, when coupled with slight height falls aloft and \nsufficient moisture and instability in place, should lead to the \ndevelopment of scattered thunderstorms later today. Expect \ninitiation over the Appalachians and the higher terrain of the \nplateau areas, but convection will eventually spread into the valley \nas time progresses, especially later on as the front draws near and \nforces things eastward. The SPC Day 1 convective outlook has been \nadjusted today to expand the Marginal risk area southward to \nencompass much of our CWA. This seems reasonable to me. The normally \nbullish NAM (with regard to moisture and instability) is actually \nhandling current surface conditions well I think, and it shows \nMLCAPE values ranging from near 2,000 J/kg in the south to around \n3,000 J/kg in the central and northern valley late this afternoon \nand early evening, with over 7 C/km lapse rates aloft as well. DCAPE \nvalues are in the 1,000 - 1,200 J/kg range. In this environment, I \nwouldn't be surprised to see some strong to severe storms capable of \nsome +60 mph winds develop. Coverage will be the limiting factor in \nour severe chances. No guidance source is overly enthusiastic with \nregards to storm coverage this afternoon, so the threat of severe \nstorms should be isolated in nature. For Friday, expect less \nafternoon shower and thunderstorm coverage than today. Instability \nwill also be less, so the threat of any strong to severe storms seem \nvery low.\n\nLooking ahead to next week, the upper ridge centered over Middle and \nEast Tennessee will mean dry conditions and a notable warming trend. \nIt doesn't appear that we will approach any record highs, but it \nwill be warm, and heat index values could climb to around or even \nslightly over 100 degrees at times. For those who care about such \nthings, August 24th was the day that average highs start their \ndecline for the year, and October 9th is the last day where record \nhighs in Knoxville reach the 90 degree mark. Don't lose hope, fall \nis on the way. I mean, for those who care about such things.",
